WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.550
Let’s look at the skin, and how psoriasis can show up.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:11.600
Psoriasis can cause raised, scaly, inflamed patches of skin.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.790
They often appear on the scalp, the elbows, or the knees.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.560
Psoriasis shows on the skin as plaques and scales.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:26.490
But it is an inflammatory disease that can affect other parts of the body, too.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.280
Most people with PsA had psoriasis first.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:36.240
But not everyone with PsA has, or will get, psoriasis.

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:42.010
A skin patch alone cannot show whether a person has joint disease.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.750
And skin and joint changes do not always happen at the same time.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.990
Your skin may look different from the pictures you see.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:58.060
If you have never been diagnosed with psoriasis, a doctor can check your skin for signs of it.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:03.230
A dermatologist is a doctor for skin, hair, and nail conditions.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:10.520
Tell your care team about new or changing patches, and about any joint symptoms.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.590
Great job! Let’s review.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.420
Skin changes are one part of PsA care.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:20.520
Your skin may look different.
